solution:
A circle of area $\pi r^2$ has radius $r$, so the two circles have radii $4$ (since $\pi\cdot4^2=16\pi$) and $10$ (since $\pi\cdot10^2=100\pi$). Their average is $\dfrac{4+10}{2}=7$, and the circle of radius $7$ has area $\pi\cdot7^2=\boxed{49\pi}$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair O=(0,0);
draw(circle(O,10),linewidth(0.8));
draw(circle(O,7),blue+linewidth(0.9));
draw(circle(O,4),linewidth(0.8));
dot(O);
draw(O--4*dir(200),linewidth(0.6));draw(O--7*dir(90),blue+linewidth(0.6));draw(O--10*dir(-30),linewidth(0.6));
label("$4$",2*dir(200),dir(110));
label("$7$",(0,4.6),E,blue);
label("$10$",8.5*dir(-30),dir(60));
label("$O$",O,dir(-95));
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

(Note that this is not the average of the two areas, $58\pi$.)

question:
Find the value of $b$ if the graph of $y=-3x+b$ passes through the given point.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] $A(-2,4)$
\item[(b)] $B(5,2)$
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Triangle $AOP$ has a right angle at $O$, with $AO=2$ and $OP=\dfrac{3}{2}$, so
\[
AP=\sqrt{4+\dfrac{9}{4}}=\dfrac{5}{2} .
\]
Since $AB$ is a diameter, $\angle ACB=90^\circ$. Triangles $AOP$ and $ACB$ are right-angled (at $O$ and at $C$) and share the angle at $A$, so they are similar, and
\[
\dfrac{AC}{AB}=\dfrac{AO}{AP}\ \Longrightarrow\ AC=4\cdot\dfrac{2}{\dfrac{5}{2}}=\boxed{\dfrac{16}{5}}.
\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair O=(0,0),A=(-2,0),B=(2,0),P=(0,1.5),C=(14/25,48/25);
draw(circle(O,2),linewidth(0.8));
draw(A--B,linewidth(0.8));
draw(A--C,linewidth(0.9));
draw(O--P,linewidth(0.8));
draw(C--B,dashed);
draw((0,0)--(0.22,0)--(0.22,0.22)--(0,0.22),linewidth(0.5));
pair u=unit(A-C),v=unit(B-C);
draw(C+0.22*u--C+0.22*(u+v)--C+0.22*v,linewidth(0.5)+gray);
dot(O);dot(P);
label("$A$",A,W);label("$B$",B,E);label("$C$",C,NE);label("$O$",O,S);label("$P$",P,NW);
label("$\dfrac{3}{2}$",(0,0.75),E);
label("$2$",(-1,0),S);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

(Check with coordinates: $O=(0,0)$, $A=(-2,0)$, $P=\left(0,\dfrac{3}{2}\right)$; line $AP$ meets the circle again at $C=\left(\dfrac{14}{25},\dfrac{48}{25}\right)$, and $AC=\sqrt{\left(\dfrac{64}{25}\right)^2+\left(\dfrac{48}{25}\right)^2}=\dfrac{80}{25}=\dfrac{16}{5}$.)

Right triangles $ABX$ and $ADY$ have $AB=AD$ and $AX=AY$, so they are congruent (hypotenuse-leg) and $BX=DY=t$, say. Then $CX=CY=1-t$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real t=sqrt(2)/2;
pair A=(0,0),B=(1,0),C=(1,1),D=(0,1),X=(1,t),Y=(t,1);
fill(A--X--Y--cycle,lightgray);
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(A--X--Y--cycle,linewidth(0.8));
label("$A$",A,SW);label("$B$",B,SE);label("$C$",C,NE);label("$D$",D,NW);
label("$X$",X,E);label("$Y$",Y,NW);
label("$t$",(1,t/2),E);label("$t$",(t/2,1),N);
label("$1-t$",(1,(1+t)/2),E,fontsize(8pt));label("$1-t$",((1+t)/2,1),N,fontsize(8pt));
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

The triangle $AXY$ is the square minus three right triangles:
\[
[AXY]=1-\dfrac {t}{2}-\dfrac {t}{2}-\dfrac{(1-t)^2}{2}.
\]
With $u=1-t$ this is $u-\dfrac{u^2}{2}$, and setting it equal to $\dfrac{1}{4}$ gives $2u^2-4u+1=0$, so $u=1\pm\dfrac{\sqrt2}{2}$. Since $0\le u\le1$, $u=1-\dfrac{\sqrt2}{2}$. Finally $XY$ is the hypotenuse of the isosceles right triangle $XCY$:
\[
XY=u\sqrt2=\sqrt2-1 .
\]
So $XY=\boxed{\sqrt2-1}$. (Check: $t=\dfrac{\sqrt2}{2}$ and $[AXY]=1-\dfrac{\sqrt2}{2}-\dfrac{\left(1-\dfrac{\sqrt2}{2}\right)^2}{2}=\dfrac{1}{4}$.)

question:
Let $D_n$ be the number of derangements of $n$ objects (arrangements in which no object is in its original place), with $D_1=0$ and $D_2=1$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Prove that $D_{n+2}=(n+1)\left(D_{n+1}+D_n\right)$ for every positive integer $n$. So in the recurrence $D_{n+2}=aD_{n+1}+bD_n$ given by this argument, $a=b=n+1$; what is $\dfrac {b}{a}$?
\item[(b)] Show that there are no constants $a,b$ (independent of $n$) with $D_{n+2}=aD_{n+1}+bD_n$ for all $n$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Let the square be $ABCD$ (clockwise) and the point $P$, with $PA=1$, $PB=5$, $PC=7$, $PD=5$.

Since $PB=PD$, $P$ lies on the perpendicular bisector of the diagonal $BD$, which is the other diagonal $AC$. As $P$ is inside the square, it lies on segment $AC$, so
\[
AC=PA+PC=1+7=8 .
\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real s=4*sqrt(2);
pair A=(0,0),B=(0,s),C=(s,s),D=(s,0),P=(1/sqrt(2),1/sqrt(2));
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(A--C,dashed+gray);
draw(P--A,blue+linewidth(0.8));draw(P--B,blue+linewidth(0.8));draw(P--C,blue+linewidth(0.8));draw(P--D,blue+linewidth(0.8));
dot(P);
label("$A$",A,SW);label("$B$",B,NW);label("$C$",C,NE);label("$D$",D,SE);label("$P$",P,dir(-45));
label("$1$",(P+A)/2,NW,fontsize(9pt));
label("$5$",(P+B)/2,E);label("$7$",(P+C)/2+(0.15,-0.15),SE);label("$5$",(P+D)/2,N);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

A square with diagonal $8$ has area $\dfrac{8^2}{2}=\boxed{32}$.

Check: the side is $4\sqrt2$, and with $A=(0,0)$, $B=(0,4\sqrt2)$, $P=\left(\dfrac{1}{\sqrt2},\dfrac{1}{\sqrt2}\right)$ one gets $PB^2=\dfrac{1}{2}+\dfrac{49}{2}=25$, as required.
